Hi Richard,

This "Hessian is singular" came at the head of the AIC list from
modSel(), so I'm not sure if it's quite the same. But I did have an
earlier problem with it as well.

When I first went to fill in all the NAs in my covariates, I added a
third "diver" factor variable ("3") to fill in for the NAs in "diver"
columns that corresponded to pools where I didn't have all 5 survey
replicates. When I did this and then tried to define some different
models, I did get "Error: Hessian is singular.  Try using fewer
covariates".  At that point, I read through past q+a and came across
the idea of adding starting values to the models, so I tried that.  To
reduce the number of covariates, I also changed my third "diver"
factor back to a "1", so that "diver" only had two factors.  These
changes worked great and allowed me to define some models and create a
fitlist without any more errors.

This new "Hessian is singular" just comes in at the top of modSel()
output...like this:

