Hi,

I'm working on some raster occupancy maps based on model averaged predictions of some excessive model mining. In general, the fitList functionality seems great, however, I would like to be able to truncate the fitList to the models up to 95% of AIC weight, and from here, generate the model-averaged coefficients so I can simply apply the coefficient values to a rasterBrick to generate predicted values.

So my questions are 1) is there a way to subset the fitList to either an AIC cut-off or to fitList sorted models 1-X? Second, coef(fittedList) generates the coefficient values for each model. Is there a method to generate the model-averaged coefficients instead of model-averaged predictions?

Matthew

From u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com  Wed Apr 18 16:19:55 2012
From: u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com (Dan Linden)
Date: Wed, 18 Apr 2012 16:19:55 +0000
Subject: Re: fitList functionality
Message-ID: <ppukVBt-d40.JeaOID7dMXsJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <ppukVBt-d40.bov6qaTEIScJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <ppukVBt-d40.bov6qaTEIScJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/ppukVBt-d40/m/JeaOID7dMXsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The following code would identify the poor models (those beyond 95% of the cumulative AIC weight) and set them to NULL in your model list:

poor.models <- modSel.object@Full$model[which(modSel.object@Full$cumltvWt>0.95),]
modelList.object@fits[poor.models]<-NULL

I'm not entirely sure why you would do this, but this modified list could be used to generate new model-averaged predictions for just the subset of models.  I tried doing this to a small set of models and found that the predictions from a modelList where all but 1 model was removed were the same as when that single model itself was specified, yet the confidence intervals were different:

> predict(fm3, type="state", newdata=newDat)
  Predicted         SE     lower    upper
1 0.8734059 0.08488826 0.6050754 0.968816
> predict(modelList, type="state", newdata=newDat)
  Predicted         SE     lower    upper
1 0.8734059 0.08488826 0.7070249 1.039787

Not sure what is going on there.  As for model-averaged coefficients, I recall reading that there are problems with trying to generate/interpret them.  I could be wrong.

